markdown <<MARKDOWN
## Reflection

<details style="margin-top: 12px; border-radius: 8px; overflow: hidden;">
  <summary style="background-color: #E4E2F8; padding: 12px 16px; cursor: pointer; font-weight: bold; color: #1F1976; font-size: 16px;">
    &#128161; How does Lesson 3's distinction between categorical and numerical data connect to Lesson 4's distinction between classification and regression?
  </summary>
  <div style="background-color: #FFFFFF; padding: 16px; border: 1px solid #ACA8EA; font-size: 16px; color: #1A1A1A; line-height: 1.6;">
    They're the same distinction, just applied to a model's output instead of raw data. A problem whose answer is categorical, like "will this customer leave a positive review?", calls for classification. A problem whose answer is numerical, like "how many customers will visit?", calls for regression. Once students can reliably tell categorical from numerical data in Lesson 3, they already have the exact skill Lesson 4's whole assessment is checking for, they just need to apply it to a question instead of a dataset.
  </div>
</details>

<hr>

<div style="background-color: #F7F7FB; border-radius: 10px; padding: 18px 20px; margin: 24px 0 16px 0;">
